      "id": "law-01",
      "subject": "law",
      "title": "NPPL(M): privileges, ratings & medical",
      "chapter": "Licence basics",
      "diagram": null,
      "body": "<h2>The NPPL with a Microlight Class Rating</h2>\n<p>The National Private Pilot’s Licence (NPPL) is a <strong>UK national, non-ICAO</strong> licence issued by the CAA. With a <strong>Microlight class rating</strong> you can fly microlights privately (not for remuneration) by day, carrying up to 4 people in total, in aircraft of up to 2,000 kg MTOM (in practice, microlights are 2-seaters).</p>\n<h3>Getting it</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Minimum age: <strong>16 for first solo, 17</strong> for the licence.</li>\n<li>Flight training with a microlight instructor: a minimum of <strong>25 hours</strong> including <strong>10 hours solo</strong> and <strong>5 hours navigation</strong> (including 3 hours solo navigation and the qualifying cross-country) for a licence without operational limitations.</li>\n<li><strong>Five written exams</strong>: Aviation Law, Human Performance, Meteorology, Navigation and Aircraft Technical (Microlight). All must be passed in the 24 months before you apply. You also need the <strong>FRTOL</strong> (radio licence) to use the radio.</li>\n<li>A <strong>General Skills Test</strong> (GST) with an examiner, plus an <strong>Aircraft (Type) oral</strong> taken within 9 months before application.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Operational limitations (the “restricted” licence)</h3>\n<p>If you qualify with fewer hours you start with <strong>operational limitations</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>No passengers (other than an instructor) until you have 25 hours including 10 as PIC, certified in your logbook.</li>\n<li>Only fly with cloud base at least <strong>1,000 ft AGL</strong> and flight visibility at least <strong>10 km</strong>.</li>\n<li>No further than <strong>8 NM</strong> from the take-off site.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The weather and distance limitations are removed after the extra navigation training and qualifying cross-country(s) (two 40 NM solo cross-countries landing at least 15 NM away, or one of 80 NM). Your instructor certifies this.</p>\n<h3>Keeping the rating valid</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The licence itself is lifelong. The <strong>class rating</strong> is valid for <strong>24 months</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Revalidate by experience</strong>: within the validity period, 12 hours including 6 hours as PIC and 12 take-offs and 12 landings. At least 6 hours must be in the 12 months before expiry, with a 1-hour flight with an instructor. Without the instructor hour the rating is endorsed “single seat only”. Or pass a skill test.</li>\n<li><strong>Renewal</strong> after it lapses: a skill test (plus an oral if it lapsed 5 years or more), with any training the instructor thinks you need.</li>\n<li><strong>Passengers</strong>: you need 3 take-offs, approaches and landings in the previous 90 days in that class/type.</li>\n<li>Moving from three-axis to weightshift (or the other way round) needs <strong>differences training</strong>, signed in your logbook.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Medical</h3>\n<p>You need either a <strong>Pilot Medical Declaration (PMD)</strong>, made online to the CAA, in which you declare that you meet the DVLA Group 1 (car) medical standard and know of no reason you are unfit, or a Class 1, Class 2 or LAPL medical certificate. You must not fly if you know or suspect you are unfit (illness, medication, after surgery, pregnancy complications, etc.).</p>\n<div class=\"confirm\">Study aid only. Figures from CAA CAP 3181 (NPPL Requirements, v2 Aug 2026). Check the current CAP and BMAA guidance.</div>\n<p class=\"meta\">Sources: CAA CAP 3181 NPPL (2026) chapters on Microlight class rating, operational limitations, revalidation and medical; ANO 2016 Part 5 and article 163 (medical declarations).</p>",
      "speakExtra": "Minimum twenty five hours including ten solo and five navigation. Operational limitations: no passengers, cloud at least one thousand feet, ten kilometres visibility, within eight nautical miles. The rating lasts twenty four months; revalidate with twelve hours, six as P I C, twelve take-offs and landings and an hour with an instructor. Ninety days, three take-offs and landings before carrying a passenger."
